 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; the server assigns each committed edit one event revision, and browsers display that revisioned view. Committed changes are pushed to connected browsers over self-hosted WebSocket connections, giving fast updates without a dependency on a realtime vendor. Subsecond updates are useful but not required; the board accepts the connection-capacity and reconnect operations this delivery route entails as owned infrastructure.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery are designed together around this choice. The change store retains replayable revisions and publishes them reliably; delivery manages socket sessions and the publication of committed revisions to connected browsers; browser sync subscribes to the stream, surfaces connection loss, and catches up on the revisions it missed after reconnecting.
 
